{"word":"link","source":"Thesaurus:link"},{"word":"nexus","source":"Thesaurus:link"},{"word":"tie","source":"Thesaurus:link"},{"word":"vinculum","source":"Thesaurus:link"}],"raw_glosses":["A form or state of connection.","(Canada, US, finance, law) The relationship between a vendor and a jurisdiction for the purpose of taxation, established for example by the vendor operating a physical store in that jurisdiction."],"glosses":["A form or state of connection.","The relationship between a vendor and a jurisdiction for the purpose of taxation, established for example by the vendor operating a physical store in that jurisdiction."],"categories":["American English","Canadian English","en:Finance","en:Law"],"tags":["Canada","US","countable","uncountable"],"id":"9c7cb68f-2d9f-4277-9443-2c6ac36049db"},{"examples":[{"text":"Sunday's election pits Move Forward and the billionaire Shinawatra family's Pheu Thai against ruling parties backed by a nexus of old money, conservatives and generals with influence over key institutions involved in two decades of upheaval in Southeast Asia's second-biggest economy.","ref":"2023 May 14, Panarat Thepgumpanat, Panu Wongcha-um, “Thailand's opposition opens up big election lead as army parties slide”, in Reuters:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[121,126]],"id":"e2ed46c7-8111-4b9a-90b5-479f468b0809"}],"links":[["connected","connected#Adjective"],["group","group#Noun"],["network","network#Noun"],["web","web#Noun"]],"categories":["English terms with quotations"],"glosses":["A connected group; a network, a web."],"tags":["countable","uncountable"],"id":"8f2dc11e-7c39-4c76-8101-144a92497a87"},{"examples":[{"text":"More than just a corporate juggernaut, Nvidia also has become an instrument of statecraft, operating at the nexus of advanced technology, diplomacy, and geopolitics.","ref":"2025 December 11, Charlie Campbell, Andrew R. Chow and Billy Perrigo, “The Architects of AI Are TIME’s 2025 Person of the Year”, in Time:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[108,113]],"id":"082e3e27-7c4f-4a03-8551-5d32bd84ae12"}],"senseid":["en:centre focus"],"links":[["centre","centre#Noun"],["focus","focus#Noun"]],"synonyms":[{"word":"hub"},{"word":"junction"}],"categories":["English terms with quotations"],"glosses":["A centre or focus of something."],"tags":["countable","uncountable"],"id":"b27406a8-e9ae-4079-a233-14c2e2ea3f9b"},{"topics":["grammar","human-sciences","linguistics","sciences"],"categories":["en:Grammar"],"links":[["grammar","grammar"],["Danish","Danish#Adjective"],["linguist","linguist"],["group","group#Noun"],["words","word#Noun"],["expressing","express#Verb"],["two","two"],["concept","concept"],["one","one"],["unit","unit"],["clause","clause"],["sentence","sentence#Noun"]],"raw_glosses":["(grammar) In the work of the Danish linguist Otto Jespersen (1860–1943): a group of words expressing two concepts in one unit (such as a clause or sentence)."],"glosses":["In the work of the Danish linguist Otto Jespersen (1860–1943): a group of words expressing two concepts in one unit (such as a clause or sentence)."],"tags":["countable","uncountable"],"id":"d3c0a573-77fc-41ea-92ab-7cdf47afc9be"},{"topics":["law"],"categories":["English terms with historical senses","en:Ancient Rome","en:Law"],"links":[["Ancient Rome","Ancient Rome"],["law","law#English"],["person","person"],["contracted","contract#Verb"],["nexum","nexum"],["obligation","obligation"],["kind","kind#Noun"],["failed","fail#Verb"],["pay","pay#Verb"],["creditor","creditor"],["compel","compel"],["work","work#Verb"],["servant","servant"],["debt","debt"],["indentured servant","indentured servant"]],"raw_glosses":["(Ancient Rome, law, historical) A person who had contracted a nexum or obligation of such a kind that, if they failed to pay, their creditor could compel them to work as a servant until the debt was paid; an indentured servant."],"glosses":["A person who had contracted a nexum or obligation of such a kind that, if they failed to pay, their creditor could compel them to work as a servant until the debt was paid; an indentured servant."],"tags":["Ancient-Rome","countable","historical","uncountable"],"id":"2dd5d94f-84e5-4c85-835f-3a16fc13afd1"}],"related":[],"synonyms":[]},"next to":{"id":"0043c109-8eb1-46ac-a534-ab6447917045","word":"next to","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"beside; alongside","word":"ao lado de"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"beside; alongside","word":"do lado de"}],"pos":"prep","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"Would you mind if I sit next to you?","bold_text_offsets":[[24,31]],"type":"example","id":"f2a1c745-a74b-4c87-8ea0-5c577794b0c0"},{"text":"Cleanliness is next to godliness.","bold_text_offsets":[[15,22]],"type":"example","id":"fcac2b31-a05d-45ab-8133-e3b88f3ac90a"}],"links":[["Beside","beside"],["alongside","alongside"],["by","by"],["adjacent","adjacent"],["near","near"]],"glosses":["Beside, alongside, by, adjacent to, or near."],"categories":["English terms with usage examples"],"id":"64a1a6aa-a755-4062-abca-d85940413e40"},{"examples":[{"text":"The living room was the main gathering spot in Fine Manor, and next to that the most used room was the dining room, where, on Sunday nights, Larry and Mabel hosted card games, penny-ante poker nights, and even bingo nights...","ref":"2006, Steve Cox, Jim Terry, One Fine Stooge: Larry Fine's Frizzy Life in Pictures : an Authorized Biography, Cumberland House Publishing, →ISBN, page 59:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[63,70]],"id":"0a0ce5d3-b843-48f2-8fb8-cf031d123ad7"},{"text":"“Next to you, I'm all Lani has,” she confided. “But you knew that already, too, right?” He should have. That message came loud and clear. “She loves flowers, did you know that?” Jennie asked.","ref":"2016, Jill Shalvis, The Bachelor's Bed, Harlequin, →ISBN:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[1,8]],"id":"c45fc543-85a9-4095-8dd9-e85e46723235"}],"links":[["after","after"],[